https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/3285"}},{"id":"11277","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7472","description":"I never have my nose plugs on when I need them.","caption":"Third Ledge","poi_name":"Third Ledge","subject":"Jimmy Lowe (xstreamkayaking.com)","author":"Jamie Burch","poi_id":"586","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/11277","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/11277"}},{"id":"8195","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"7473","description":"Jimmy Lowe punches the hole on the third ledge. ","caption":"Third Ledge","poi_name":"third ledge","subject":"Jimmy Lowe (xstreamkayaking.com)","author":"Greg Simpson","poi_id":"190","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/8195","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/8195"}},{"id":"2647","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"4991","description":"","caption":"Third Ledge","poi_name":"Third Ledge","subject":"Kevin Miller","author":"Will Reeves","poi_id":"190","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/2647","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/2647"}},{"id":"877689","ext":"jpeg","post_id":"877966","description":"This was taken looking upstream at the Canoe Eating Rock","caption":"Canoe Eating Rock rapid","poi_name":"Canoe Eating Rock","subject":"","author":"RiverSasquatch","poi_id":"195","photo_date":null,"updated_at":null,"url":{"medium":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/medium/877689","large":"https://api.americanwhitewater.org/image/images/big/877689"}}],"detail":{"id":"471","permitUrl":"","permitInfo":"","description":"**RIVER DESCRIPTION**\n\nThe Upper Hooch (not section 3 - thats over on the Chattooga) is a classic beginner canoeing and kayaking stream. Its relatively short, has an easy shuttle, decent scenery and fun rapids. It can be run at just about all levels from ridiculously scrapy to screaming high flood stage, depending on the boaters skills of course. At low flows its a class two stream. Around 3-4 feet on the Leaf Gage its easy Class III. Above that its class 3/4 with some huge holes.\n\nOnce upon a time during the winter of '96-97 a group of us ran the Upper Hooch at 13 feet (about 14,500 cfs) on the Cornelia Gage. Lots of moving strainers. Two massive holes in First Island rapid that were of equal size to the top holes in Insignificant on the Gauley. The rock outcrop on the right at second ledge was a giant pourover. Third ledge was washed out. We ran it twice before the Hall and White County rescue squads closed down the river. Probably a good thing--three other groups put on and hiked/swam off. Lots of canoes wrapped around trees that day.\n\n**Flow Info: (NOTE - we have begun using the Leaf Gage rather than the Cornelia gage on this page as of 2/15/15 because it better reflects the flow experinced on this reach)**\n\nFor the Leaf Gage, consider the following advice:\n\n- <1.5 = Very Low\n- 1.5-2 = Low but runnable\n- 2-3 = Good for a wide variety of paddlers (II-II+), surfing begins to get good.\n- 3-4 = Class III whitewater, good surfing, good flow range\n- >4 = High water, additional skill required (III/IV), not good for beginners.\n\n**Parking Info:** The put-in and take-out is Georgia State Park land, and requires either a daily pass ($5) or an annual pass.\n\n[Wildwood Outfitters](http://www.wildwoodoutfitters.com) has an outpost at the take-out and will shuttle you and your boat to the put-in for $7. The outpost is usually closed during the winter, late October to early March.\n\n**Directions:**\n\nFrom Atlanta, go north on I-85 16 miles to I-985. Continue 42 miles up I-985. Then take a left at Duncan Bridge Road, GA 384 at the light. The take-out is about 4 miles down Duncan Bridge Rd.\n\nTo get to the put-in, continue west on Duncan Bridge Rd, take a right on GA 254, and another right on GA 115. The put-in will be the first stream you come to. Parking is very limited at the put-in.\n\nWhitewater Home Companion Vol. 1, William Nealy\n\nAppalachian Whitewater : The Southern States","averageGradient":"25","maxGradient":null,"length":"4.1","correlations":[{"reachID":"471","isPrimary":true,"correlationDetails":{"metric":"levelFT","belowRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableRangeComment":null,"mediumRunnableRangeComment":null,"highRunnableRangeComment":null,"aboveRecommendedRangeComment":null,"lowRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"highRunnableAdjustedDifficulty":null,"beginLowRunnable":"1.8","beginMediumRunnable":null,"beginHighRunnable":null,"endHighRunnable":"5"},"gaugeInfo":{"name":"CHATTAHOOCHEE RIVER NEAR LEAF, GA","gaugeSource":"USGS","gaugeSourceIdentifier":"02331000","gaugeFetchStatus":"ACTIVE","latestFlowReading":{"value":"189","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"latestStageReading":{"value":"1.73","dateTime":"2026-05-12T02:45:00.000Z","qualifier":"NORMAL"},"location":{"latitude":"34.5770833","longitude":"-83.6360278"},"externalSourceLinks":{"sourceLink":{"url":"https://water.noaa.gov/gauges/02331000","text":"NOAA"},"secondaryLink":{"url":"https://waterdata.usgs.gov/monitoring-location/02331000/#parameterCode=00060&period=P7D","text":"US Geological Survey"}}},"migrationErrorExplanation":"Previous Flowbands: \nLow Runnable Range (R0-R5): 1.8 - 2 levelFT Adjusted Difficulty: IstandoutII Comment: You will leave a lot of plastic on rocks and get stuck in places.    Any lower is abusive. \r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nMedium Runnable (R4-R6): 3 - 4 levelFT Adjusted Difficulty: III Comment: \t\t\t\tGood  whitewater level (III)\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\nHigh Runnable (R6-R9): 4 - 5 levelFT Adjusted Difficulty: IIItoIV Comment: \t\tSuitable for advanced paddlers (III/IV)\r\n\r\n\r\n\nMedium Runnable (R4-R6): 2 - 3 levelFT Adjusted Difficulty: IIplus Comment: \t\tGood all round level for many paddlers (II-II+)\r\n\r\n\r\n\n\nMigration Errors: 